inated and that is  that when you get your package from  various schools you can kind of play one  against the other now you have to do so  as the student him or herself mommy and  daddy you can't call so what you want to  do is you want to say to the college hey  ABC University has given me a package  worth this much could you match it can  you actually help me out what kind of  work-study is there going to be again  you've got to figure out whether this  degree is going to translate  into an a career and you can pay that  debt down and actually achieve your  future financial goals and we know  millions of Americans will have to  resort to taking out a student loan  there's nothing wrong with that but what  are some of the things they should look  out for so the big trap that people fall  into is just taking on gobs of debt like  Megan did assuming the best-case  scenario now I want you to assume the  worst case scenario and say to yourself  hey I'm going to be having a job it's  not gonna make that much money so think  of it this way the first job you get out  of college let's say you make $30,000  that's the total amount of borrowing  that should be done and for parents I  don't care whether your kid wants to be  the most sophisticated artist artistic  collector in the world or academic do  not delay any retirement plans on your  part to put those kids through school  you as a family have to be realistic you  have to all be able to afford for these  decisions college degrees pay off but  they don't do so if you have the  overhang of thousands and thousands of  dollars of debt either for the parents  the grandparents or the kids think about  the long-term ripple effects indeed feel  great to have you on thanks so much  everybody to go buy your book and if you  have questions about student loan debt  head to the CBS this morning Facebook  page now and Jill we'll continue the  conversation and answer your questions  there
 

